From fabb0eabbc72f53bb14f6ba38a26aec52c2c6d09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:36:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] player information: the colour of the film in the words of
 the interface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The colour row of the player information (hades.video.color, on the
overview and the video pages) was made in English by the window system:
"BT.2020 · PQ · limited range", "BT.709 (assumed) · not given · ...". The
names of the standards are the same in every language, but "not given",
"assumed", "full range", "limited range" and "linear" were shown in
English in Spanish too, where every other word of the page is translated.

The window system now keeps the parts of the colour (primaries, whether
Kodi chose them, transfer function, range) and says them when it
publishes the page, with Estuary's strings as $ADDON[skin.estuary n]
takes them for the rows (ColourWordsOf(): "not given" is #31834, already
used by the page; "assumed", "full range", "limited range" and "linear"
are new, #32007-#32010, in en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx). Where a string
is missing the word stays in English. PLAYERINFO::ColourText() takes the
words (ColourWords, English by default).

Host test (outside the tree): test_info.cpp checks ColourText() with
Spanish words (it does not build against the header before: there is no
ColourWords); 6 mutants (each word back to English, "linear" not
replaced) are killed. t_colour.py checks that the words of the header,
the ids that ColourWordsOf() takes and Estuary's strings agree, with a
translation in each Spanish file. WinSystemGbm.cpp,
WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp and DVBridgeGLES.cpp compile with the
commands of the last build.
